  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA cold front will march through our area Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning. A soaking stratiform rain is close to\ndefinite for the forecast area as precipitation onset arrives \nfrom west to east starting Wednesday morning and lasting into \nThursday morning. Rain could be moderate to heavy at time,\nespecially in the afternoon and evening. Isolated rumbles of \nthunder are also possible with forcing from the front, but \nscattered to widespread thunderstorm activity remains very low.\n\nFlooding concerns remain very low after several dry days. Any\nvery localized flooding that does occur would likely be the \nresult of drains being stopped up from leaf litter. A large \nchunk of the forecast area, namely our Ohio, Kentucky, central \nand western West Virginia counties will likely be in the range \nof 0.50\" and 1.00\" of rainfall. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nCooler temperature trends arrive this weekend behind the front.\nThe lowlands will average temperatures in the mid 50s to the low\n60s Saturday through Monday. The mountains will remain in the\nmid 40s to 50s. Overnight lows are projected to dip down into\nthe 30s to lower 40s each night. This will lead to frost and\nfreeze concerns which could require product issuance across our\nactive growing season counties in southern and central West \nVirginia, Kentucky, and southwest Virginia, especially Sunday \nand Monday mornings. \n\nDrier weather for most is anticipated this weekend, however\nchances for upslope rain showers will exist through the weekend\nacross the mountains with the post-frontal, northwesterly flow.\nArea wide chances for showers are expected Sunday night and\nMonday, with the chance for some flakes across the higher \nridges of the northeastern mountains of West Virginia. \nTemperatures will not be quite cold enough to warrant \naccumulations.",
